  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Structural confidence and sequence-based scores are being tested as selection filters, exposing failure modes outside training distributions.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is whether learned sequence and structure models remain reliable when the evaluation distribution departs from training. In lanmodulin REE-binding design, structure confidence (pTM, ipLDDT) is reported to be a poor selection filter, and a function-trained model on measured Tb affinity is proposed instead. ProtCOVER applies family-level conformal calibration to variant effect predictors on unseen protein families, with density-ratio reweighting under descriptor shift. The antibiotic resistance classifier reaches AUROC 0.982 on CARD but fails on ResFinder sequences non-redundant with its training data, so cross-database redundancy is a relevant factor. MADM and Calpha extend the scope: MADM combines per-residue structural descriptors with PLM embeddings for eight antibody developability assays, while Calpha routes LLM residue states to a SimpleFold structure head for single-chain folding. The new element is explicit validation of selection and calibration, not accuracy alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
